---
id: org.dxos.spec.review
name: DSL.Review
version: 1.0
extends:
  - org.dxos.spec.core
---

The review dialect. A review is a separate document that judges how far the code meets a spec, item
by item. It is written by an agent (the `spec-review` skill) or a person, and lives beside the spec
it judges: `PLUGIN.mdl` → `PLUGIN.review.mdl`. Each new review replaces the previous file; git keeps
the history. The spec viewer reads the latest review and marks each judged item with its status.

A review is evidence, not a decision: it never changes the spec, and it adds no changelog entry.

## Extensions

| Term      | URI                         |
| --------- | --------------------------- |
| `verdict` | `org.dxos.mdl.verdict@1.0` |

## Document

Frontmatter names what was reviewed and against what:

```yaml
---
spec: github:dxos/dxos/packages/plugins/plugin-file-system/PLUGIN.mdl
commit: 64dcf3f44a541fdf0b3e55803f38d214e76a6279   # the code the review read
date: 2026-10-22
by: agent:claude-code                              # same actor forms as the changelog
---
```

The body opens with a short summary, then one `verdict` per reviewed item.

## Statuses

| Status     | Meaning                                                                  |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `done`     | The code does what the item says.                                        |
| `untested` | The code does it, but no test exercises it.                              |
| `partial`  | Some of what the item says is done; the note says which part is not.     |
| `diverged` | The code does something different from what the item says.               |
| `missing`  | Nothing in the code does it.                                             |

The spec viewer shows beside each judged item whether code covers it (its `evidence:`, or "no code")
and whether tests exercise it: `tested` when the verdict names `tests:` or is `done`, `untested` for
any other status but `missing`, where there is no code to test.

## Tests and scenarios

A `scenario` or a `test` block describes behaviour to exercise, so it is judged on whether
automation exercises it, not on whether code implements it:

| Status     | For a `scenario` or `test`                                                  |
| ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `done`     | An automated test runs it end to end; `tests:` cites that test.             |
| `partial`  | A test covers some of its steps or `then:` clauses; the note says which.    |
| `diverged` | A test runs something different from what it describes.                     |
| `missing`  | Nothing runs it (a manual QA run is not automation).                        |

`untested` does not apply. `tests:` names the test files that exercise it and is required for
`done`, `partial` and `diverged`; `evidence:` may name the code it drives. The viewer shows
`tested`, `partly tested` or `not tested` beside it, with a chip per test file.

## Walkthrough

When an item is implemented in more than one place, the verdict carries a `walkthrough:`: 1 to 5
segments, in reading order, that say where each part is implemented. Each segment is one
sentence followed by the `github:` key of the code it describes; the viewer shows the sentence
above the declaration the key names, linked to its file. Keep each sentence to what the code
does for this item, not how it works line by line.

- A `type` needs none: its one declaration is the hover card of its code chip.
- An `op` walks its declaration (the schema), then each handler.
- A `feat` or `req` walks the pieces in the order a request passes through them.

An item with a single implementation gets no walkthrough.

A `feat` with nested `req` items is judged through its requirements; its status is the worst of
theirs. Judge a `feat` directly only when it has no requirements.

## Definitions

```mdl
ext verdict
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.verdict@1.0
  desc: |
    How far the code meets one spec item. The identifier is the item's id in the reviewed spec
    (`F-1.2`, `Scene`, `openDirectory`); the inline prose is the verdict in one line.
  fields:
    status: done | untested | partial | diverged | missing
    evidence?: RefList          # github: keys of the code that supports the verdict, with #Symbol
    tests?: RefList             # github: keys of the test files that exercise the item, without #
    walkthrough?: ProseList     # 1-5 items `- <one sentence>. github:<key>`, when there is more than one implementation
    note?: Prose                # what is missing or different; required unless status is done
  syntax: |
    verdict F-1.2: The picker opens on the action.
      status: done
      evidence: [github:dxos/dxos/packages/plugins/plugin-file-system/src/util.ts#openDirectoryPicker]
      tests: [github:dxos/dxos/packages/plugins/plugin-file-system/src/util.test.ts]
```
